<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Montbéliard</h1><p class="st-meta">1042 CE – 1793 CE (approx.) · capital Montbéliard</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A French-speaking Lutheran enclave ruled by the dukes of Württemberg for four centuries before France annexed it in 1793.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Montbéliard guarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfort-gap">Belfort Gap</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>. Around 1042 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-of-mousson">Louis of Mousson</a>, who also held Bar through his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophie-of-bar">Sophie</a>, ruled it as count. Later houses of Montfaucon and Chalon held it, all within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles</a> and so in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1397 the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henriette-of-montbeliard">Henriette of Montbéliard</a> was betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-iv-count-of-wurttemberg">Eberhard IV of Württemberg</a>, and the county passed to the Württemberg dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillaume-farel">Guillaume Farel</a> preached there in 1524–1525, and under the Württemberg rulers it later became Lutheran, an unusual Protestant enclave between Catholic <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-county-of-burgundy">Franche-Comté</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-duke-of-wurttemberg">Duke Frederick I</a> ruled it from 1581 and brought in the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schickhardt">Heinrich Schickhardt</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French troops occupied the county several times, and in 1793 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a> annexed it. Württemberg recognised the loss in 1796. The region kept its Lutheran heritage and later became the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peugeot">Peugeot</a> industrial dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-montbeliard.jpg" alt="County of Montbéliard" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ivan Sache &amp; Pascal Vagnat · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ferrette.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1042 CE – 1793 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Montbéliard</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis of Mousson, Henriette of Montbéliard, Frederick I of Württemberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Montbéliard, Mömpelgard, Principality of Montbéliard</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=county-of-montbeliard" aria-label="County of Montbéliard on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4839 512 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Montbéliard lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5159 768L5229 694M5159 768L5212 730M5159 768L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5159 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5159 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Montbéliard. <a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=county-of-montbeliard">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
